---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import retentionData from '../data/retention.json';

// Shared with the per-operator and per-province pages so a retention figure
// is only ever written down once.
const retention = retentionData.entries;


---
<Base
  title="The rules"
  titleFull="ALPR Data Retention Rules by Province — Canada, US, UK"
  description="ALPR retention spans 3 minutes to forever depending on jurisdiction. The full comparison: Canada, the US, and the UK — with sources.">
  <h1>The rules: retention is a setting, not a standard</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The same technology keeps your plate read for 3 minutes in New Hampshire, 24 hours in
    Peel Region, 7 days in Toronto, a year in the UK — and forever in most US states.
    Five orders of magnitude, decided by a dropdown menu.
  </p>
  <nav class="pills">
    <a href="#retention">Retention</a>
    <a href="#oversight">Oversight</a>
    <a href="#ontario">Ontario</a>
  </nav>

  <div class="note">
    <strong>Two neighbouring services, same province, same technology:</strong> Peel Regional
    Police keeps non-matching scans for 24 hours. Toronto Police keeps them 7 days. Nothing
    about the tech requires either number — retention is an admin setting, not a board vote.
  </div>

  <h2 id="retention">How long they keep the plates of innocent drivers</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Jurisdiction</th><th>Non-hit read retention</th><th>Authority</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {retention.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{row.hl ? <strong>{row.j}</strong> : row.j}</td>
            <td>{row.hl ? <span class="tag hot">{row.r}</span> : row.r}</td>
            <td style="color: var(--muted)">{row.a}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="oversight">Three models of oversight</h2>
  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>🇨🇦 Canada — commissioner-led</h3>
      <p>No ALPR statute anywhere. Privacy commissioners (OPC, IPC Ontario, OIPC BC, CAI Québec)
      set doctrine through investigations and guidance. The template: BC's 2012 ruling that keeping
      non-hit data is unlawful. Result: short retention, no vendor-run national search network,
      and no Flock — the company has zero Canadian police contracts.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>🇺🇸 United States — patchwork + litigation</h3>
      <p>~16 states have statutes; ~34 leave it to agency discretion. Enforcement comes from
      lawsuits (the Norfolk Fourth Amendment case is at the Fourth Circuit) and state AGs —
      California sued El Cajon, Illinois caught Flock giving CBP access. Since Feb 2025,
      50+ communities have cancelled Flock contracts.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>🇬🇧 UK — everything, for a year</h3>
      <p>The National ANPR Service: ~14,000 cameras, 100M+ reads per day into one national
      database, 12-month retention — with no statutory basis, and its dedicated oversight
      office recently abolished. The former commissioner called it possibly "one of the largest
      data gatherers of its citizens in the world."</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ontario">Ontario: the spread, funded provincially</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>What</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Toronto</strong></td><td>Entire marked fleet (~560–600 vehicles) scans — 1.25M+ plates/day capacity. 2026 budget adds $5.6M for "AI Operationalization."</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Brampton (Peel)</strong></td><td>Genetec fixed cameras at key intersections (50 per early reports; Genetec's release says 200).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>York Region</strong></td><td>$255K expansion; every camera has ALPR, feeding a real-time operations centre on Axon Fusus.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Waterloo Region</strong></td><td>Combined CCTV + ALPR live June 23, 2026; Kitchener, Cambridge, North Dumfries next. Funded by Ontario's Guns, Gangs and Violence Reduction Strategy.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Sudbury</strong></td><td>31 equipped vehicles + fixed ALPR at 16 locations (provincially funded).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Province-wide</strong></td><td>Ontario has signalled it is assessing highway ALPR. The commitment appears in the government's announcement accompanying <a href="https://www.ola.org/en/legislative-business/bills/parliament-44/session-1/bill-119" rel="noopener">Bill 119, Protecting Ontario's Streets and Communities Act, 2026</a> — the bill's own text contains no ALPR provisions. OPP: all frontline vehicles equipped since 2023.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <strong>Does any of this actually reduce crime?</strong> The randomized trials say no
    deterrence; the best fixed-network study finds an 11% auto-theft drop. The full studies,
    hit rates, and wrongful-stop record: <a href="/evidence">the evidence →</a>
  </div>

  <style>
  </style>
</Base>
